Build **Wireworm**, a fixed-shooter arcade game with a charged-terrain discharge
system that runs entirely in the browser.

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. Create the game there.

## Your starting point

A starter project is already in place at `{{workspace}}`: a `package.json` that
pins **Playwright** as a dev dependency, and an `assets/` folder of the game's
sprite art. Its dependencies have already been installed for you — `node_modules/`
is populated and Playwright's headless **Chromium** is downloaded — so you can
build right away (see "Playwright and Chromium" below for what they are there for).
Build on this project rather than replacing it: keep the `package.json`, add your
framework, bundler, and a `build` script on top of it, and keep the lockfile in
sync (see below).

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## Build requirements

Deliver a complete, polished, playable game, not a tech demo. The **hard
requirements** — real graphics rendered from the provided sprite art, no backend
or credentials, the palette and type, a `README.md`, the game states, and the
`window.__wireworm` debugging and automation API and debug overlay
(`specs/instrumentation.md`) — are stated in full in `specs/overview.md` (and the
files it points to). That specification is authoritative; build to it rather than
to a summary here. Three things bear restating, because the harness depends on
them:

- **The fixed build/serve interface.** The project stays a **Node project** built
  with only Node.js and npm-installed dependencies. Extend the provided
  `package.json` at `{{workspace}}` and **commit a `package-lock.json`** (run
  `npm install` after you change dependencies so the lockfile matches). `npm ci`
  then `npm run build` must produce the static site — with no other manual step —
  into one of `dist/`, `build/`, or `out/` at the project root, with an
  `index.html` at the root of that directory. Serving that directory as-is must run
  the game, and it must work **at any base path** (it is played back from a per-run
  sub-path, so any URL the build constructs at runtime must be page-relative, never
  a leading `/`; for a bundler, set a relative base such as `base: './'`).
  `specs/overview.md` states this in full; follow it exactly.
- **The provided art.** The game's sprites are seeded under `{{workspace}}/assets/`;
  render from that art rather than drawing your own, and load it with page-relative
  URLs so it resolves from the served sub-path. `specs/assets.md` is the
  authoritative contract for every asset's layout, how to composite and animate it,
  and the short list of things you do draw in code.
- **The reference screenshots.** The `reference/` folder under `{{workspace}}` holds
  screenshots that are **illustrative examples**, not targets to reproduce, of the
  title, gameplay, and game-over screens. They show one way the screens can look;
  design your own menus and layout from the specification, in the palette and type
  the specs define.

## Playwright and Chromium

The project's **Playwright** and its headless **Chromium** are installed, and the
container has network access, so you can drive the built game in a real browser.
Using them to validate your build as you go — serving it (including from a non-root
sub-path, so you can confirm the sprite assets still load), driving a run, and
exercising the `window.__wireworm` API — is a good way to catch problems, but
whether and how far to do so is your call. (You will separately need them for the
proof-of-implementation captures that `specs/proof.md` asks for.)

Whenever you do launch the browser, use the project's own Playwright: import it from
your project (for example `import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the
project-local CLI with `npx playwright`. There is **no** global Playwright on
`PATH` — it lives in your project's `node_modules`, like any other dependency.
Launch Chromium with the `--no-sandbox` argument — this container has no Chromium
sandbox, so a default launch will fail.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a **git repository** — it was initialized before you started,
with the starter files in its first commit. Your finished implementation is
published directly from this repository's commits, so work you leave uncommitted is
**not** published. When the build is complete and verified, commit everything: run
`git add -A` then `git commit`, and confirm `git status` reports a clean working
tree with nothing uncommitted or untracked. A `.gitignore` already excludes
`node_modules/` and the build output (`dist/`/`build/`/`out/`), so a clean tree
does not mean committing those — but it does mean committing your source, the
provided `assets/`, your `package-lock.json`, your `README.md`, and the `proof/`
artifacts.
